{
  "area": "traversal-vehicles",
  "description": "A vehicle as an entity the simulation still understands (US-2 and US-4 of 122-traversal-and-travel). Each case carries the WHOLE input — the authored vehicle with its mode, owner, seat count and action atoms, its per-playthrough state, the actor, where the ACTOR is, and the verb — so a harness in any language reproduces it with no defaults table and no world file. What is deliberately absent from every case is the whole of what an engine has: there is no speed, no acceleration, no turning circle, no buoyancy and no mesh, because a vehicle’s entire traversal meaning is the mode it lends whoever is driving it (docs/mechanic-predicates.md §12) and speed and handling are the host’s (§11). `expected.resolution` is what core decided before the rules layer was asked; the refusal is the FIRST that applies in VEHICLE_REFUSALS order (unknown, elsewhere, full, not_aboard, occupied, redundant, forbidden). `unknown` never appears because it belongs to the decision layer — a pure function is handed a vehicle — and `forbidden` never appears because permissibility is the rules layer’s: this module supplies `anothers`, the fact a theft norm is authored over, and an unowned cart is `false` because core does not invent a prohibition. `expected.next` is the state the verb produced and is `null` for a refused verb, so a caller cannot apply a transition core did not afford; `expected.delta` is the { retract, assert } fact delta in the 110 vocabulary, computed by DIFFING the two states rather than from which verb ran, so a state restored from a save produces the same facts as one reached by playing. Occupant lists are canonically sorted. Regenerate with `npm run traversal-corpus`.",
